WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
Your gut lining is a sheet just one cell thick.

2
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.580
How does it keep pulling glucose into your blood, even when the gut holds less than

3
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.580
the blood?

4
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:15.247
Its top, the apical face, looks into the gut.

5
00:00:16.527 --> 00:00:21.147
Its sides and base, the basolateral face, look toward its neighbours and the blood.

6
00:00:22.487 --> 00:00:24.107
Each face carries different proteins.

7
00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:31.047
On the apical face sits a carrier called SGLT1, a sodium-glucose carrier.

8
00:00:32.447 --> 00:00:33.807
Think of it as a turnstile.

9
00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:38.207
Two sodium ions and one glucose pass through it together.

10
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.500
Sodium is crowded outside the cell and sparse inside, and the inside is negative.

11
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.660
So sodium falls inward, downhill.

12
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.300
That fall, not the turnstile itself, pays the fare for glucose to climb.

13
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.660
Inside, glucose piles up a little.

14
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.020
It leaves through a free exit door on the basolateral face.

15
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.500
That door is a passive carrier: it spends no energy, and glucose runs out downhill.

16
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.680
It is GLUT2 in the standard account.

17
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:14.273
Each glucose brought two sodium ions in.

18
00:01:15.393 --> 00:01:21.233
The sodium pump, also on the basolateral face, spends ATP to throw sodium back out.

19
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:23.633
That keeps the slope steep.

20
00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:25.913
Now run the clock.

21
00:01:27.213 --> 00:01:31.513
The gut's glucose gauge falls to the blood's level, then keeps falling below it.

22
00:01:32.773 --> 00:01:34.053
Glucose is moving uphill.

23
00:01:38.117 --> 00:01:39.477
What sets the direction?

24
00:01:40.817 --> 00:01:43.637
Under these gut gradients, the carriers' places do.

25
00:01:44.897 --> 00:01:50.497
A sodium-paid turnstile on top and a free exit door underneath bias glucose from gut

26
00:01:50.497 --> 00:01:51.497
to blood.

27
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.670
Now look between the cells.

28
00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.910
Neighbours are joined near the top by a seal.

29
00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:02.550
Try a thought experiment: loosen that seal enough to let glucose slip through.

30
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.250
Would the gut still take up glucose?

31
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.320
It depends on the gut.

32
00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.700
Between the cells, glucose just follows its own gradient.

33
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.660
Early in a meal, the gut holds more than the blood, so the open seal adds to

34
00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:23.660
absorption.

35
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.240
Late in the meal, the gut holds less than the blood.

36
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.940
Now the leak runs backwards.

37
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:36.120
Glucose slides back out between the cells, and the gut level stalls at a floor it

38
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.120
cannot sink below.

39
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:40.433
Tighten the seal, and the floor drops.

40
00:02:41.693 --> 00:02:45.753
In this thought experiment, what the sheet absorbs is what it carries through the

41
00:02:45.753 --> 00:02:48.373
cells, minus what leaks back between them.

42
00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:50.867
Here is the one idea.

43
00:02:52.187 --> 00:02:57.147
Under the usual gut gradients, where the carriers sit biases glucose flow from gut

44
00:02:57.147 --> 00:03:00.807
to blood. The seal does not orient that pathway.

45
00:03:02.027 --> 00:03:05.747
It decides what can slip between the cells, and so what can leak back.

46
00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:09.287
Our glucose leak was a thought experiment.

47
00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:14.627
What the real seal lets through, and how it is measured, is in the text below.
